.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH iron-vault-forget-machine 1  "Iron Vault" "Iron Vault Manual"
.SH NAME
iron\-vault\-forget\-machine \- Check that a retired machine\*(Aqs recipient key is gone from the vault, then print how to decommission the machine. Run it on the vault that stays, with the key `recipient list` showed for the machine. It exits 1 with `machine\-not\-forgotten`, telling you to run `recipient remove`, while the key is still enrolled. It warns when the vault no longer meets the independence rule or no recovery check has run lately. It changes nothing and runs no TPM tool: you run every step it prints
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBiron\-vault forget\-machine\fR [\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fIRECIPIENT\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Check that a retired machine\*(Aqs recipient key is gone from the vault, then print how to decommission the machine. Run it on the vault that stays, with the key `recipient list` showed for the machine. It exits 1 with `machine\-not\-forgotten`, telling you to run `recipient remove`, while the key is still enrolled. It warns when the vault no longer meets the independence rule or no recovery check has run lately. It changes nothing and runs no TPM tool: you run every step it prints
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-admin\-socket\fR \fI<PATH>\fR
Use the admin socket at PATH instead of this installation\*(Aqs, for example one forwarded over SSH. Every client command takes it; `serve` refuses it, because the service always binds its own installation\*(Aqs socket
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help
.TP
<\fIRECIPIENT\fR>
The retired machine\*(Aqs `age1...` recipient key
